       "text": "I won't enter this one as written. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the maximum labeled dose, and I can't think of an indication where that is appropriate. Even with normal kidney and liver function, it puts the patient at real risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular harm. Confirming the order doesn't change that risk, and you haven't given a clinical rationale.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **Enter a dose within the maximum.** Give me the dose and frequency, such as 800 mg PO three times daily (2,400 mg/day) or 600 mg PO four times daily (2,400 mg/day), and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Discuss it with your attending or a clinical pharmacist.** If there is an unusual reason for this dose, they can help you decide whether to order it. They can also enter it through a pathway that includes pharmacy verification.\n- **Tell me what you're trying to treat.** If pain or inflammation control is the goal, I can help you build a regimen with a multimodal approach, such as scheduled acetaminophen along with ibuprofen at a safe dose."
